[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Hibernate Interceptors and Event Listeners (Entity Lifecycle Hooks)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When building enterprise applications with Hibernate, tracking entity state changes, automatically populating audit fields (like created_at, updated_at), and implementing cross-cutting business rules are common requirements. Hibernate provides two powerful mechanisms for intercepting entity lifecycle events: Interceptors and Event Listeners."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Interceptors"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Interceptor interface allows applications to inspect and/or modify the property values of a persistent object before it is loaded, saved, updated, or deleted. Interceptors are typically tied to a specific Session instance."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing a Custom Interceptor"},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"public class AuditInterceptor extends EmptyInterceptor {
    @Override
    public boolean onSave(Object entity, Serializable id, Object[] state, String[] propertyNames, Type[] types) {
        for (int i = 0; i < propertyNames.length; i++) {
            if ("createdAt".equals(propertyNames[i])) {
                state[i] = new Date();
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
}"},{"order":7,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Event Listeners"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Hibernate's event architecture is more robust and fine-grained than the Interceptor mechanism. It isSessionFactory-scoped rather than Session-scoped, meaning listeners are registered globally and execute for all sessions."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing a Pre-Update Event Listener"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"public class CustomPreUpdateEventListener implements PreUpdateEventListener {
    @Override
    public boolean onPreUpdate(PreUpdateEvent event) {
        Object entity = event.getEntity();
        if (entity instanceof Auditable) {
            // Modify state array directly
            // event.getState() and event.getPersister()
        }
        return false;
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"heading","text":"JPA Lifecycle Annotations (Alternative Hooks)"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"For standard JPA applications, Hibernate supports callback annotations directly on entities, which are much simpler to implement for basic auditing and lifecycle management."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@PrePersist and @PostPersist: Executed before and after an entity is inserted into the database."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@PreUpdate and @PostUpdate: Executed before and after an entity is updated."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@PreRemove and @PostRemove: Executed before and after an entity is deleted."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@PostLoad: Executed after an entity has been loaded into the current persistence context or refreshed."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Example Using JPA Annotations"},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
public class User {
    @Id
    private Long id;
    private String username;
    private LocalDateTime updatedAt;

    @PreUpdate
    public void preUpdate() {
        this.updatedAt = LocalDateTime.now();
    }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"heading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep interception logic lightweight to avoid slowing down database operations."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid performing nested database queries inside interceptors or event listeners to prevent unexpected recursion and performance degradation."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prefer JPA lifecycle annotations for standard auditing tasks, and fall back to Hibernate Event Listeners only when global, advanced framework-level interception is required."}]
